  --fast-max <n>          Cap the primed kill set (default 25).

For the mutations-file format, and what each guard refuses: git mutate -h
"""

HELP_TEXT = r"""git mutate -- break the code on purpose, and report whether the tests noticed.

For each mutation: guard it, snapshot the files it touches, apply it, run the test command,
restore the tree, and classify every failing test as an assertion kill (a test checks this
behaviour) or an error kill (the mutation merely broke the code, proving nothing).

Usage:
  git mutate - [name...]                  Read TOML from stdin; run all or named mutations.
  git mutate --check - [name...]          Read TOML from stdin; run guards only, no tests.
  git mutate <mutations-file> [name...]   A file path is also accepted.
  git mutate --recover                    Restore from a stale sweep's snapshot; cmp-verify.

  --cmd <shell command>   Test command, instead of the pytest hook in .pre-commit-config.yaml.
  --env <name>            Test ecosystem: pytest (default), mvn, go, rust, dotnet.
  --assertion-pattern <re> Extra regex that marks a message as an assertion kill.
  --timeout <seconds>     Per-mutation timeout (default 900).
  --no-fast               Run the full suite for every mutation (see THE FAST TIER).
  --fast-cmd <template>   Subset command; '{}' is replaced by the test ids.
  --fast-max <n>          Cap the primed kill set (default 25).

MUTATIONS are TOML. Prefer a heredoc into 'git mutate -': it is visibly one-use, leaves no
temporary file to clean up, and cannot become a stale mutation suite by accident:

  git mutate - <<'TOML'
  [[mutation]]
  name = "history-never-sent"
  file = "src/autorp/claims.py"
  old = '''        "history": _history_anchor(played),'''
  new = '''        "history": [],'''
  TOML

'name', 'file', 'old' and 'new' are all required; 'tests' is optional. Arguments after '-' select mutations by
their 'name'. A file path remains supported when another tool already produced the TOML.

'file' is relative to the repo root. Anchors are matched byte-for-byte against the file's
bytes: write them as literal ''' strings, so nothing needs escaping and the leading
indentation is part of the anchor. TOML drops a newline immediately after the opening ''',
which is what lets a multi-line anchor start on its own line:

  old = '''
        if not allowed:
            raise PermissionError(name)'''

A mutation may also carry 'tests': the ids you expect to fail, tried first by the fast tier.

  [[mutation]]
  name = "history-never-sent"
  tests = ["tests/test_claims.py::test_history_is_sent"]

It is a HINT, never a filter. It only changes which tests run FIRST; a hint that reddens
nothing falls through to the full suite exactly like any other subset, so a wrong or stale
hint costs time and never a verdict. Its real value is the first mutation of a sweep, which
otherwise pays a full run before anything is primed. Note that pytest collects NOTHING when
any id fails to match, so one stale id disables the tier for that mutation -- the sweep says
so at the end rather than letting it pass as a silent slowdown.

Naming 'tests' also buys a check on yourself. If a test you named does not fail, the sweep
says so under EXPECTATIONS NOT MET -- including when the mutation was killed anyway, by some
other test. That case is the one worth having: the verdict reads "killed by assertion", the
sweep exits 0, and without this line the fact that the test you believed defends this
behaviour does not would never surface. It never changes a verdict or the exit status: "this
behaviour is undefended" and "your guess about which test defends it was wrong" are different
claims, and folding them together would make a clean sweep unreadable.

A bare id for a parametrized test names every parametrization of it, and the expectation is
met when ANY of them fails by assertion -- "this test defends the behaviour" -- whatever the
others did. Only failures are visible, so "every case failed" could not be checked anyway;
to expect one case in particular, name it: 'test_foo[case1]' matches only itself.

SEVERAL EDITS AT ONCE. A two-part change -- add something here, drain it there -- stays one
mutation, applied and reported as one: list [[mutation.edit]] tables instead of the three
keys, each table carrying its own 'file', 'old' and 'new'. One [[mutation]] may not use both
spellings -- carrying [[mutation.edit]] *and* a top-level 'file' is refused, not guessed at.

THE GUARDS. Each refuses that one mutation loudly, names the offending edit, and lets the
rest of the sweep run. A refusal is "not measured", never a survivor:
  - every edit's 'old' must occur exactly once in its file, with no privileged first edit
  - 'new' must differ from 'old'
  - once every edit is applied, the file must actually differ from the tree
  - an anchor sitting in a '#' comment, or inside a Python docstring, is refused: mutating
    prose reddens nothing, and would report a defended behaviour as undefended

Use 'git mutate --check -' with the same heredoc form to put the input through every guard
in seconds, running no tests and touching nothing.

THE FAST TIER. Mutations in one sweep cluster -- they are written against one behaviour, so
the same few tests kill most of them. Once a test has killed by assertion, later mutations run
just that set first. An assertion kill there ENDS that mutation: running more tests can only
add reddened tests, never remove one, so the full suite's verdict is provably the same
'killed'. Only the row list is shorter, and the report says so.

Nothing else short-circuits. No assertion kill, a line the classifier cannot read, or a
compile failure all fall through to the full unnarrowed suite -- so every FINDING still comes
from a complete run. A badly primed set costs time, never a false survivor.

The set is re-priced against a CLEAN tree whenever it changes, and anything red there is
excluded: a test that passes in the suite but fails alone must not be credited as a kill.

It turns itself off, loudly, when it cannot narrow: a non-pytest --env, a command that is not
pytest-shaped, or one that already names its own paths (appending ids to 'pytest tests/' would
widen the run, not narrow it). Use --fast-cmd to drive it yourself, --no-fast to be rid of it.

THE INPUT IS EPHEMERAL. Feed it on stdin for one invocation and forget it. Anchors are
coupled to the code's current text while tests are coupled to its behaviour, so a kept
mutations file rots -- and the dangerous rot is not the anchor that stops matching, it is
the one that comes to match a comment.

RUN IT UNPIPED. A sweep is slow -- one full test run per mutation, plus a baseline -- and it
prints one line per mutation as that mutation finishes, so an unpiped run is a live progress
report. A grep/head filter blocks until the sweep ends, making a run that is stuck, timing
out or refusing every anchor look exactly like one that is working. 'head' is worse: it can
SIGPIPE the sweep mid-mutation, the one way to leave a tree needing --recover.

Exit status (precedence when several apply: 4 > 3 > 2):
    0  every selected mutation was measured and killed by at least one assertion
    1  usage or environment error -- nothing was measured
    2  findings: a mutation reddened nothing, or was reddened only by errors
    3  not measured: a mutation was refused, timed out, failed to compile, or produced
       test output this classifier cannot read
    4  restore verification failed -- THE TREE MAY STILL BE MUTATED
  130  interrupted; the tree was restored
A survivor is a finding (2), never a tool failure.
"""


class Exit(Exception):
    """Unwind to the single cleanup path, which restores the tree before the process dies."""

    def __init__(self, code):
        self.code = code


def usage():
    sys.stderr.write(USAGE_TEXT)


def help_text():
    sys.stdout.write(HELP_TEXT)


def die(msg):
    print("%s: %s" % (SELF, msg), file=sys.stderr)
    raise Exit(1)


def emit(msg=""):
    print(msg, flush=True)


def emit_err(msg=""):
    print(msg, file=sys.stderr, flush=True)


